# UAE Health Insurance Pricing Model

I built this model to demonstrate how a health insurer in the UAE 
would approach end-to-end actuarial pricing — from raw policy data 
all the way through to renewal rate recommendations and regulatory 
compliance checks. Everything is built in R and structured the way 
I would expect to see it in a real pricing team.

## What the model does in plain English

At its core the model answers three questions:

**What should we charge?**
A GLM framework estimates expected claim frequency and severity for 
each member based on their age, sex, nationality, health condition, 
diagnosis type, plan and network. Multiplying frequency by severity 
gives the pure premium — the technical cost before loadings.

**Are we charging enough right now?**
The model compares actual retained claims against current gross 
premium to produce loss ratios at portfolio, segment and policy 
level. With a portfolio loss ratio of 94.5% against a target of 
85%, the model correctly identifies the book is running above target.

**What should we charge at renewal?**
Each policy gets a credibility-weighted renewal rate using 2024 
actual experience blended with the GLM estimate. Rate changes are 
capped between -10% and +35% and checked against UAE regulatory 
minimum premiums for 2026.

## Key numbers
- 268,801 members across 25,000 policies
- Portfolio loss ratio: 94.5% on current premium basis
- GLM validated on 2024 holdout — A/E ratio 0.993
- Poisson dispersion ratio 1.022 — Poisson correctly retained
- 4 diagnostic graphs and 11 CSV outputs

## Why I built it this way

A few decisions worth explaining:

**Current vs future premium separation** — I keep two separate 
premium columns throughout. Current premium is what the policyholder 
pays in 2025 with no trend applied. Future premium applies the 
11.3% medical trend and is used for renewal pricing, stress testing 
and IFRS17 projections. Mixing these up is a common mistake that 
makes loss ratios look misleadingly good or bad.

**Multi-year training data** — Instead of pricing on one year of 
data I generate 2022, 2023 and 2024 historical cohorts by sampling 
and trend-deflating the 2025 base. The GLM trains on 2022-2023, 
validates on 2024 and prices on 2025. This is how a real insurer 
would approach it.

**UAE market specifics** — The model reflects the actual regulatory 
structure including DHA EBP minimum premiums, UAE federal basic 
premium, three DHA network tiers, five nationality groups with 
separate frequency and severity relativities, C-section shock at 
the UAE 38% rate, and spouse minimum age of 23 with child 
nationality following the father.

## Key assumptions
| Assumption | Value | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Medical trend | 11.3% | WTW 2026 Middle East Survey |
| EBP minimum premium Dubai | AED 620 (2025) / AED 635 (2026) | DHA circular |
| Federal basic premium | AED 310 (2025) / AED 320 (2026) | UAE Federal Decree Jan 2025 |
| IFRS17 discount rate | 3.65% | CBUAE base rate Jan 2026 |
| Reinsurance retention | AED 35,000 per member | Treaty design |
| Reinsurance ceded share | 90% of excess | Treaty design |

## How to run it
1. Open RStudio and set this folder as your working directory
2. Run Package setup.R first — this installs and loads everything needed
3. Then run each file in the order listed below, or use 
   source("main.R") to run the whole model at once
4. All outputs will appear in UAE_Health_Pricing_Model_Outputs/

## Files in order
| File | What it does |
|---|---|
| Package setup.R | Installs tidyverse, broom and MASS |
| Governance and Assumptions.R | Every assumption in one place — change a value here and it flows through the whole model |
| Generate 2025 base portfolio.R | Creates 25,000 synthetic policies |
| Member expansion - 2025 portfolio.R | Expands into members — spouse opposite sex to employee, child nationality follows father |
| Diagnosis and Prior claim.R | Assigns diagnosis groups and condition flags |
| Frequency effect's and claim count.R | Simulates claim counts using Poisson with nationality and region effects |
| Severity effect and gross claim amount.R | Simulates claim costs including C-section shock |
| Benefit Design and Reinsurance.R | Deductibles, coinsurance, claim caps and reinsurance |
| Data Quality + conversion to Health model.R | Filters and factors the data for GLM |
| Generating historical Cohorts 2022 - 2024.R | Builds training data by deflating 2025 claims back in time |
| Train & Validate & Price split.R | Splits by year — 2022-23 train, 2024 validate, 2025 price |
| Frequency GLM.R | Poisson GLM with age-sex interaction and overdispersion check |
| Severity GLM.R | Gamma GLM with diagnosis group ranking validation |
| Apply pricing to 2025 portfolio.R | Produces current and future gross premium with regulatory floors |
| Portfolio and Segment profitability.R | Loss ratios, segment flags and two graphs |
| Credibility renewal pricing.R | Square-root credibility renewal with 2024 prior year experience |
| Anti Selection and Diagnosis mix.R | Identifies which segments are attracting bad risks and what is driving costs |

## Graphs
| Graph | What it shows |
|---|---|
| graph_01_lr_heatmap.png | Loss ratio by plan type and region — green to red scale |
| graph_02_current_vs_future_premium.png | Side by side current vs future premium by plan type |
